# Long-Running Agent Harness (Cursor)

This rule distills **[*Effective harnesses for long-running agents*](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-harnesses-for-long-running-agents)** (Anthropic, 2025-11-26) and **[*Harness engineering: leveraging Codex in an agent-first world*](https://openai.com/index/harness-engineering/)** (OpenAI, 2026-02-11) into **enforceable Cursor behavior**.

---

## Mandatory workspace conventions (this repository)

These override generic defaults whenever applicable.

### Prefer the visual companion (Cursor Canvas)

When the deliverable is a **standalone analytical or data-heavy artifact**—metrics breakdowns, investigations, audits, architecture reviews with categorized findings, multi-row tables users need to inspect, timelines, charts, MCP-sourced dashboards where **the structured data itself is the output**—**prefer opening a Cursor Canvas** (visual companion / `.canvas.tsx`) beside chat instead of pasting giant markdown tables or wall-of-text blocks into the reply.

- If you are about to emit a large markdown table primarily for consumption as a visual report, **use a canvas** unless the user asked for a different surface (e.g. “put this only in Slack,” “embed in existing file”).
- Follow the **Canvas** workflow: eligibility → single file under workspace `canvases/*.canvas.tsx` → imports only `cursor/canvas` → embed data inline—see Cursor Canvas skill/policy when creating or editing canvases.

Do **not** use a canvas for quick fixes, narrow debugging, PR-only edits, or when the canvas would duplicate a user-specified target system.

### User-facing language

Unless the user **explicitly** asks for another language, write **all natural-language assistant replies in chat in Chinese (简体中文)**. Technical identifiers, file paths, code, cited URLs, and quoted error messages remain as-is.

